'''A team preparing a public exhibition kept missing internal deadlines. Members worked hard, but each interpreted "finished" differently. One considered a text finished when the first draft was written. Another assumed it had already been checked and approved. As a result, later tasks repeatedly began with incomplete material.

At the next meeting, the coordinator asked everyone to describe what they needed from the previous stage. The designer needed approved text, not a draft that might change substantially. The printer needed files in a specific format and a confirmed quantity. Volunteers needed instructions that reflected the final layout.

The team defined a small set of completion criteria and assigned one person to confirm each handover. They also reduced the number of display panels rather than pretending the original schedule remained realistic. This disappointed some members, but it made the remaining commitments more credible.

After the exhibition, the team reviewed the process. The biggest improvement had not come from asking people to work faster. It came from making expectations visible and negotiating the scope before the final week. Clear communication became part of completing a task, rather than something to do only after the practical work was over.''',
'''Why were deadlines missed despite effort?|Members understood finished differently.|Nobody did any work.~The exhibition had already closed.|Faltaban criterios compartidos.
What did the designer need?|Approved text.|Any unfinished notes.~Only a printing quantity.|El estado de entrega importaba.
Why reduce the number of panels?|To make remaining commitments realistic.|To prevent visitors attending.~To avoid every discussion.|Se ajusta alcance al tiempo.
What was the main improvement?|Clear expectations and negotiated scope.|Working faster without coordination.~Ignoring handovers.|La comunicación forma parte de la tarea.''',
'''In an interview, a candidate described herself as organised, reliable and good at teamwork. The interviewer asked for an example. She then explained how a volunteer event had nearly run out of chairs, how she contacted a nearby centre and how her team arranged transport before guests arrived.

The example made the qualities easier to assess. It also allowed the interviewer to ask what the candidate would do differently next time. She said she would confirm equipment earlier rather than rely on last-minute problem-solving. The answer showed both a contribution and an ability to learn from it.''',
'''Why did the interviewer request an example?|General qualities needed concrete evidence.|The candidate had already explained everything.~Teamwork was irrelevant.|Los adjetivos solos informan poco.
What problem did the candidate address?|A shortage of chairs.|A missing job application.~An empty timetable.|El incidente da contexto.
What improvement did she identify?|Confirm equipment earlier.|Avoid helping the team.~Wait until every event ends.|Se extrae una acción futura.
What did the answer demonstrate?|Contribution and reflection.|Perfect performance with no problems.~A result achieved entirely alone.|Reconoce al equipo y aprendizaje.''',
'''Before we finish, let me check the actions we have agreed. I will contact the venue by Wednesday. Priya will prepare the participant list, provided that the registrations close tomorrow as planned. We have decided to postpone the printed brochure until the programme is confirmed. Could everyone send me one concern that might affect their task? We can discuss these on Thursday instead of discovering them on the day of the event. If your workload changes, please tell the group early. It is better to negotiate a smaller commitment than to make a promise that you cannot keep.''',
'''When will the speaker contact the venue?|By Wednesday.|After the event.~Only if a brochure is printed.|By señala fecha límite.
What is being postponed?|The printed brochure.|All registration.~Every team meeting.|Depende de confirmar programa.
What should members do if workload changes?|Inform the group early.|Keep it secret.~Wait until the deadline passes.|Permite renegociar a tiempo.''',
'Escribe un correo de 140–190 palabras tras una reunión: acuerdos, responsables, plazos, condición y una pregunta pendiente.',
'''Hi everyone,

Thank you for today's planning meeting. I am writing to confirm the main actions so that we all work from the same information. I will contact the venue by Wednesday and check that the smaller room is available for the workshop.

Priya will prepare the participant list once registration has closed. This should happen tomorrow, but she will let us know if the deadline needs to change. Ben has agreed to check the equipment and report any missing items by Thursday afternoon.

We decided to postpone printing the brochure until the programme is confirmed. In the meantime, we can share a short digital notice that clearly marks the schedule as provisional.

One question remains open: who will welcome participants at reception? Please reply if you can take responsibility for that task. If any commitment above is unrealistic, raise the issue before Thursday's review. It will be easier to adjust the plan now than to deal with conflicting expectations on the day.

Best,
Lina''',
